iding a private-label application to  a specific chain of fitness centers or  to end-users through the variety of  channels that they do that that that  users are able to go and get access to  google maps with all of its location  location-based services but in the  context of this application experience  so I think that what we're seeing very  successful within the API space is where  you're able to basically cross pollinate  this experience by sharing data and  process some of the other forms of this  slide are things that I think you're  going to see more of as we look out in  the future of different forms of  ecosystems one of the things I wanted to  leave you with at the end of this  presentation is this print this session  that I just did is the first part of  what we're calling the connected  business track here at at Google next  tomorrow Chet Kapoor CEO of apogee at  Google is going to be talking about this  vision of a connected business platform  and how all of these different  ecosystems power different types of  business models and some of these  business models that we see are actually  these clearinghouses and marketplaces  for digital services and we'll get into  a lot more depth in that keynote  tomorrow so let me wrap this up so when  we talk about API first and we talked  about API centric thinking hopefully  this presentation has given you kind of  this perspective of thinking about this  outside in approach even if you're api's  are predominantly being used for  internal purposes it makes sense if your  scope of reuse is wide enough if you are  working with other development teams  that are sort of beyond arm's length  where you're not scrubbing with them or  you're not regularly interacting with  them once  you need to suddenly be thinking about  your API consumers as customers now if  your API is are designed for for being  API zhis products and you definitely are  gonna have to think about this the  competitiveness of your API the success  of